#: ../../../tutorials/advanced/earth_relief.rst:22
msgid "Plotting Earth relief"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/advanced/earth_relief.rst:24
msgid ""
"PyGMT provides the :func:`pygmt.datasets.load_earth_relief` function to "
"download the Earth relief data from the GMT remote server and load as an :"
"class:`xarray.DataArray` object. The data can then be plotted using the :"
"meth:`pygmt.Figure.grdimage` method."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/advanced/earth_relief.rst:30
msgid ""
"import pygmt\n"
"from pygmt.params import Axis"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/advanced/earth_relief.rst:38
msgid ""
"Load sample Earth relief data for the entire globe at a resolution of 1 arc-"
"degree. Refer to :func:`pygmt.datasets.load_earth_relief` for the other "
"available resolutions."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/advanced/earth_relief.rst:43
msgid "grid = pygmt.datasets.load_earth_relief(resolution=\"01d\")"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/advanced/earth_relief.rst:52
msgid "Create a plot"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/advanced/earth_relief.rst:54
msgid ""
"The :meth:`pygmt.Figure.grdimage` method takes the ``grid`` input to create "
"a figure. It creates and applies a color palette to the figure based upon "
"the z-values of the data. By default, it plots the map with the *turbo* CPT, "
"an equidistant cylindrical projection, and with no frame."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/advanced/earth_relief.rst:61
msgid ""
"fig = pygmt.Figure()\n"
"fig.grdimage(grid=grid)\n"
"fig.show()"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/advanced/earth_relief.rst:71
msgid ""
":meth:`pygmt.Figure.grdimage` can take the optional parameter ``projection`` "
"for the map. In the example below, ``projection`` is set to ``\"R12c\"`` for "
"a 12-centimeters-wide figure with a Winkel Tripel projection. For a list of "
"available projections, see :doc:`/techref/projections`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/advanced/earth_relief.rst:78
msgid ""
"fig = pygmt.Figure()\n"
"fig.grdimage(grid=grid, projection=\"R12c\")\n"
"fig.show()"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/advanced/earth_relief.rst:90
msgid "Set a colormap"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/advanced/earth_relief.rst:92
msgid ""
":meth:`pygmt.Figure.grdimage` takes the ``cmap`` parameter to set the CPT of "
"the figure. Examples of common CPTs for Earth relief are shown below. A full "
"list of CPTs can be found at :gmt-docs:`reference/cpts.html`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/advanced/earth_relief.rst:98
msgid "Using the *geo* CPT:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/advanced/earth_relief.rst:102
msgid ""
"fig = pygmt.Figure()\n"
"fig.grdimage(grid=grid, projection=\"R12c\", cmap=\"gmt/geo\")\n"
"fig.show()"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/advanced/earth_relief.rst:112
msgid "Using the *relief* CPT:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/advanced/earth_relief.rst:116
msgid ""
"fig = pygmt.Figure()\n"
"fig.grdimage(grid=grid, projection=\"R12c\", cmap=\"gmt/relief\")\n"
"fig.show()"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/advanced/earth_relief.rst:128
msgid "Add a colorbar"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/advanced/earth_relief.rst:130
msgid ""
"The :meth:`pygmt.Figure.colorbar` method displays the CPT and the associated "
"z-values of the figure, and by default uses the same CPT set by the ``cmap`` "
"parameter for :meth:`pygmt.Figure.grdimage`. The ``annot`` parameter sets "
"the annotation interval, the ``label`` parameter sets the x-axis label, and "
"the ``unit`` parameter sets the y-axis label."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/advanced/earth_relief.rst:138
msgid ""
"fig = pygmt.Figure()\n"
"fig.grdimage(grid=grid, projection=\"R12c\", cmap=\"gmt/geo\")\n"
"fig.colorbar(annot=2500, label=\"Elevation\", unit=\"m\")\n"
"fig.show()"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/advanced/earth_relief.rst:151
msgid "Create a region map"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/advanced/earth_relief.rst:153
msgid ""
"In addition to providing global data, the ``region`` parameter of :func:"
"`pygmt.datasets.load_earth_relief` can be used to provide data for a "
"specific area. The ``region`` parameter is required for resolutions at 5 arc-"
"minutes or higher, and accepts a list in the form of [*xmin*, *xmax*, "
"*ymin*, *ymax*]."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/advanced/earth_relief.rst:158
msgid ""
"The example below uses data with a 10 arc-minutes resolution, and plots it "
"on a 15-centimeters-wide figure with a Mercator projection and a CPT set to "
"*geo*. ``frame=Axis(annot=True)`` is used to add a frame with annotations to "
"the map."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/advanced/earth_relief.rst:164
msgid ""
"grid = pygmt.datasets.load_earth_relief(resolution=\"10m\", region=[-14, 30, "
"35, 60])\n"
"fig = pygmt.Figure()\n"
"fig.grdimage(grid=grid, projection=\"M15c\", frame=Axis(annot=True), "
"cmap=\"gmt/geo\")\n"
"fig.colorbar(annot=1000, label=\"Elevation\", unit=\"m\")\n"
"fig.show()"
msgstr ""

